1. Nf3 d5 2. d4 Bf5 {Already a surprise. This move has the advantage of rapid, simple development with the idea of a quick e5.} 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bd6 5. Bxd6 Qxd6 6. c4 Nf6 (6... Qb4+ 7. Qd2 {leaves White with the preferable endgame.} )7. Nc3 O-O 8. Rc1 Nc6 9. c5 Qe7 {Black was developing his pieces rapidly and was aiming for an early ...e5. With 9 c5 I wanted to keep the centre closed and to control the dark squares. With 9...?e7 Black is aiming for ...e5 even if he has to sacrifice a pawn.} (9... Qd7 {would have led to more quiet variations, e.g.} 10. Bb5 Bg4 11. Qa4 Bxf3 12. gxf3 Rfe8 13. f4 )10. Bb5 Bg4 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. Qa4 Bxf3 13. gxf3 e5 14. Ne2 {[#]} e4 15. f4 Ng4 $5 {This move seems best and leads to interesting complications. So much for my attempt at keeping this a positional battle.} 16. Qxc6 Qh4 17. Ng3 Qh3 {The threat is 18...?g2 19 ?c2 ?xe3.} 18. Rc2 Rfd8 {Now 18...?g2 would have been answered with 19 ?e2.} 19. Kd1 $1 {The King runs for safety to the other side! Unclear is} (19. Qxc7 h5 20. Qe7 h4 21. Nf5 Qg2 22. Qg5 Qxh1+ 23. Kd2 g6 24. Qxg4 Qf1 25. Ne7+ Kf8 26. Qxh4 Qd3+ )Rac8 (19... Nxh2 20. Qxc7 )(19... Qg2 20. Kc1 Nxf2 (20... Nxh2 21. Rd1 Ng4 22. Nf5 Nxf2 23. Qxc7 $16 )21. Kb1 Qf3 22. Rf1 )20. Kc1 Qg2 21. Kb1 h5 (21... Nxh2 22. Rcc1 Nf3 23. Nf5 Kh8 24. Rh5 $18 )(21... Kh8 22. Qa6 $16 )22. Qa6 {Threatening ?f1 leaving Black with no compensation for the pawn.} Nxh2 23. Rcc1 Rb8 $1 (23... Nf3 24. Rxh5 {and doubling rooks on the h-file should lead to a win.} )(23... Ng4 24. Rcf1 Nxf2 25. Rhg1 Qh2 26. Nxh5 $1 Qxh5 27. Rxf2 $16 {when it will be difficult for ?lack to defend his pawns and avoid the attack along the g-file.} )24. Qe2 Ng4 {Both 24...?f3 and 24...h4 lead to a winning game for White.} (24... Nf3 25. Rxh5 )(24... h4 25. Nf5 )25. Rcf1 g6 26. Nxh5 $1 {[#] The point of 22 ?e2.} Qf3 (26... gxh5 27. Rfg1 Qf3 28. Qxf3 exf3 29. Rxh5 )27. Ng3 Kg7 28. Rh4 {Exchanging Queens gives Black compensation because the pawn on f2 is vulnerable to attack.} f5 (28... Qxe2 29. Nxe2 f5 30. Nc3 c6 31. Nd1 Rh8 32. Rfh1 {and Black has no compensation for the pawn.} )29. Qa6 $1 {The Queen returns to the attack and threatens a penetration at c6 or e6. Note that if 29...?xf2 30 ?xf5+ leads to mate. Other moves are not as strong.} Nxe3 {[#]} (29... Nf6 30. Qe6 Re8 31. Nxf5+ gxf5 32. Rg1+ $18 )(29... Rh8 30. Qc6 $1 Rxh4 31. Qxc7+ Kf6 32. Qd6+ Kg7 33. Qxb8 $18 )(29... Nxf2 30. Nxf5+ {and it's mate in seven. [6 says Fritz5]} )30. Rh3 $3 {A quiet move, but the best one. Other possibilities were not as good, and some were downright bad. For example:} (30. Nh5+ gxh5 31. fxe3 Rxb2+ $1 32. Kxb2 Rb8+ 33. Kc1 (33. Ka1 Qxe3 $19 )Qg2 34. Re1 Qb2+ 35. Kd1 Qf2 36. Qe2 Qxh4 $17 )(30. fxe3 Qxg3 31. Rfh1 Rh8 32. Qe6 (32. Qxa7 Rxh4 33. Qxc7+ Kh6 )Rxh4 33. Qe7+ Kg8 34. Qxh4 $14 )(30. Qe6 $2 Rxb2+ $1 31. Ka1 Nc2+ 32. Kxb2 Rb8+ 33. Kc1 Qa3+ {and it's mate in five.} )(30. Rg1 Rxb2+ $1 (30... Ng4 31. Nxf5+ )31. Ka1 Ng2 (31... Ng4 32. Rgh1 )32. Nxf5+ (32. Qe6 Rxa2+ )gxf5 33. Qh6+ Kf7 34. Qh7+ Ke8 35. Qg6+ Kd7 36. Qxf5+ Kc6 37. Qf6+ Kb7 38. Qxd8 Qc3 $19 {Is another example how White could go wrong.} )Rxb2+ {Best! Other possibilities were} (30... Nxf1 31. Nxf5+ gxf5 32. Qh6+ Kf7 33. Qh7+ Ke8 34. Qg6+ Kd7 35. c6+ {and its mate in two.} )(30... Qg2 31. Nh5+ gxh5 32. Rxe3 h4 33. Ree1 $18 Kf7 34. Rg1 Rxb2+ 35. Kxb2 Qxf2+ 36. Ka3 $18 )(30... Qg4 31. Nh5+ gxh5 32. Rxe3 h4 33. Ree1 Kf7 34. Rg1 Qh5 35. Rg5 Qh7 36. Reg1 Rg8 37. Qc6 $18 )31. Ka1 {This is better, and definitely more interesting, than the endgame that follows} (31. Kxb2 Nc4+ 32. Qxc4 dxc4 33. Nxf5+ gxf5 34. Rxf3 exf3 35. Kc3 Re8 )Rdb8 {Giving up the queen is the best chance to hold the game.} (31... Rxf2 32. Nxf5+ Nxf5 33. Rxf3 Rxf3 34. Rg1 $18 )(31... Qg2 32. Qc6 (32. Rfh1 Qxh3 $5 33. Rxh3 Rdb8 34. Nxf5+ Nxf5 35. Rb3 $18 )Rdb8 (32... Qxh3 33. Qxc7+ Kh6 34. Qxd8 )33. Qxc7+ Kg8 34. Qh7+ Kf8 35. Qh8+ Kf7 36. Rh7+ Ke6 37. Qe5# )(31... Qg4 32. Qc6 (32. Rfh1 Qxh3 $1 )Qxh3 33. Qxc7+ Kh6 34. Qxd8 $18 )32. Nxf5+ (32. fxe3 Rb1+ $1 33. Rxb1 Rxb1+ 34. Kxb1 Qd1+ $11 {Leads to perpetual check.} )Nxf5 (32... gxf5 33. Qh6+ Kf7 34. Qh7+ Ke8 35. Qg6+ Kd8 36. Rh8+ Kd7 37. c6+ Ke7 38. Rh7+ {leads to mate.} )33. Rxf3 exf3 34. Rg1 Ne7 35. f5 (35. Re1 Re2 36. Rxe2 fxe2 37. Qxe2 {was also good.} )c6 36. fxg6 Re2 37. Rh1 {[#] The clearest way to a win.[37 Qa3 line] In this pos...} (37. Rh1 {In this position, Black resigned. Several possible continuations were:} Rf8 (37... Rbb2 38. Rh7+ Kf8 (38... Kxg6 $2 39. Rxe7 )39. Rf7+ Ke8 40. Qxa7 $18 )(37... Kxg6 38. Qd3+ Nf5 39. Qxf3 Rbb2 (39... Re4 40. Qh5+ Kf6 41. Rg1 Rxd4 42. Qg5+ Ke6 43. f4 Rf8 44. Qg6+ Kd7 45. Rb1 )40. Qh5+ Kf6 41. Qh8+ Ke6 42. Qc8+ Kf6 43. Qxc6+ Kg5 44. Qxd5 $18 )(37... Rh8 38. Rxh8 Kxh8 39. Qxa7 Kg7 40. Qc7 $18 Re4 41. a4 Kxg6 42. a5 Kf7 43. a6 $18 )38. Qxa7 Kxg6 39. Qc7 $18 )({Taking the a-pawn offers Black some counterplay:} 37. Qa3 Rf8 38. Qxa7 Rh8 39. Qc7 Ra8 )1-0

