1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. Qg4 O-O 8. Bd3 f5 9. exf6 Rxf6 10. Bg5 Rf7 11. Qh5 g6 12. Qd1 Nbc6 13. Nf3 Qf8 14. O-O c4 15. Be2 h6 16. Bc1 Bd7 17. a4 Qg7 18. Ba3 g5 19. Bd6 Ng6 20. Qd2 b6 (20... Rd8 21. h3 Bc8 22. Bg3 Nf4 23. Bd1 Rdf8 24. Re1 {Now 24...b6 would have transposed to this game but} Rf5 25. Nh2 h5 26. Rb1 Kh8 27. Nf3 h4 $14 {but Black managed to survive in Timmerman-Anton, same event (CM 6/2001).} )21. h3 Rd8 22. Rfe1 Bc8 23. Bg3 Rdf8 24. Bd1 Nf4 25. Ne5 Nxe5 26. Rxe5 Qg6 27. a5 b5 28. Bg4 Nh5 29. Bh2 $5 {White invites the following simplification.} (29. Bxh5 Qxh5 30. Rb1 Bd7 31. f3 {also came into consideration.} )Rxf2 30. Bxh5 Qxh5 31. Qxf2 Rxf2 32. Kxf2 {Diagram [#] Your computer may tell you Black has an edge here, but it cannot correctly evaluate the superiority of White's bishop on h2 which has enormous potential compared with the black bishop hemmed in by its own pawns. Rittner does see the danger and decides to transform the structure before White can lock it.} b4 $5 33. cxb4 Qh4+ 34. Ke3 g4 {A decision point for White now. Computers may like ?f4 here but White wants to protect d4 witha pawn to anchor his centre and free his king from defensive duties.} 35. c3 $1 gxh3 36. Rg1 $1 {The white pieces begin to work together again.} Kh7 37. gxh3 Qxh3+ 38. Bg3 Bd7 39. Kf2 Qg4 40. Rge1 Be8 {Why does Black give up the pawn instead of defending passively? Rittner apparently believes that his queen will be overcome by the rook pair in the long run if his bishop cannot assist in creating counterplay.} 41. Rxe6 Qf5+ 42. Kg1 Bg6 43. R6e3 Qc2 44. Be5 h5 45. Rg3 h4 46. Rg2 Qd3 47. Rf1 Bf5 (47... h3 {might be tougher, although it would eventually lose the h-pawn with the black bishop in a more passive situation.} 48. Rf8 Kh6 49. Rh8+ Bh7 50. Bf4+ Kh5 51. Rg3 Qd1+ 52. Kh2 Qe2+ 53. Kxh3 Qf1+ 54. Rg2 )(47... Qxc3 $4 48. Rf8 )48. b5 h3 49. Rgf2 Bc8 {Diagram [#]} 50. a6 {White prepares the decisive creation of a passed pawn.} Qxc3 ({Black also loses after} 50... Kh6 51. b6 axb6 52. a7 Bb7 53. Rf3 h2+ 54. Kxh2 Qe2+ 55. R1f2 Qe4 56. Rf6+ )51. b6 $1 axb6 52. a7 $1 Qa3 (52... Bb7 53. Rf7+ )53. Rf8 Kh6 54. Kh1 Qxa7 55. Rxc8 1-0

