1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. g3 dxc4 5. Bg2 Nc6 {In recent years this move contends in popularity with 5...?e7, 5...c5, and 5...a6.} 6. O-O Rb8 7. Nc3 a6 8. e4 Be7 9. Qe2 $5 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qxd4 11. Rd1 Qc5 12. e5 ({In the same tournament, Burger played against me} 12. Be3 Qb4 13. Bf4 e5 $5 14. Bxe5 Bg4 15. Bf3 Bxf3 16. Qxf3 Qb6 17. Bf4 Qc6 18. Nd5 {and the game was drawn on the 27th move.} )Nd7 13. Bf4 ({In the game J.Klinger-U.Bönsch, Lugano 1989, White played the risky continuation} 13. Ne4 Qxe5 14. Bf4 Qa5 15. Qxc4 e5 16. Bd2 Qb6 17. Be3 c5 18. Nc3 Nf6 19. Qa4+ Bd7 20. Rxd7 {but was not successful.} )O-O 14. Rac1 Qa5 $1 {Otherwise comes 15 ?e4 and ?xc4. Too risky seemed to me 14...b5 15.?e4 and the black queen will be chased.} 15. h4 $1 Re8 {Necessary prophylaxis. In the expectation of a white attack, the moves ...?f8 and ...?f8 must be prepared.} 16. h5 h6 17. Be4 Nf8 ({After} 17... b5 18. Rxd7 $1 Bxd7 19. Bb1 {and ?c2 the white attack achieves at least a draw.} )18. Bb1 b5 {Diagram [#] After some preventive moves, finally Black can make a principal move. The c4-pawn is defended and Black plans ...?b7. White must play "either - or".} 19. Bxh6 $5 gxh6 20. Rd4 {The attack mechanism 21 ?g4+ ?h8 22 ?e3 ?g5 23 ?xg5 hxg5 24 ?xg5 is prepared with a possibility of perpetual check. Maybe the idea 25.h6 and 26.Qg7 mate can give more.  The next move obtains more space for a black defence. I analysed 20...Bg5 too, but very dangerous seems me for example [see the line ***] ... Therefore 20...?g5 is maybe the best move, but I was not able to see all consequences of 20...f5 and 20...Bg5. Finally I have decided according to my way thinking (or feeling).} f5 $1 ({The following variation is not in the analysis in C.Sach but JF replied when I asked him about this.} 20... Bg5 $145 {but very dangerous seems to me, for example,} 21. Rg4 Kh8 22. f4 (22. Rxg5 hxg5 23. Qe3 f6 $1 24. exf6 b4 25. Ne4 )Be7 23. Ne4 {with a long-lasting initiative. I do not remember all my analyses. Maybe I did not see all consequences of [22 Rg5 line]} )21. exf6 Bxf6 22. Rg4+ Bg7 {At this point in the game Umansky emigrated from Russia to Germany. My moves had been received by Umansky in Piatigorsk or Saratov usually after 15-20 days (the record was 31 days). His cards reached me a few days quicker than my moves to him.  Now the transmission time was reduced to 5 days (sometimes 3 days). The game had taken two years to reach move 22 but now it speeded up markedly.} ({After} 22... Kf7 23. Ne4 Bh8 24. Qe3 {or} (24. Qf3+ {the white attack continues.} ))23. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 24. Qe5+ (24. Qg4+ {Possibly} Kh8 25. Qf4 {24.Qg4+ Kh8 25.Qf4 could lead to a draw by repetition. White prepares the following manoeuvre of his rook.} )Kg8 ({Another move is} 24... Kf7 {The computer of my friend Robert Tibenský promised a win for Black after} 25. Qf4+ Kg8 26. Qg4+ Kh8 27. Qf4 b4 28. Qf6+ Kg8 29. Bg6 Qg5 30. Bf7+ ({But I have found quickly} 30. Ne4 $3 Qxf6 (30... Qxc1+ 31. Kh2 {with perpetual check.} )31. Nxf6+ {and 32 ?xe8 with an equal ending, or (30...Qc1 line)} )Kh7 31. Qxg5 hxg5 32. Bxe8 bxc3 33. bxc3 {(But 30 Ne4 line)} )25. Rd1 $1 {Diagram [#] A logical move, better than 25 ?f6 or 25 ?e4. White threatens ?-d4-g4+.} Bb7 $1 26. Rd4 Bf3 27. Rf4 Qb4 $1 28. Rxf3 Qd6 (28... Qe7 29. Rf4 $1 (29. Ne4 Nd7 30. Qxc7 Rbd8 )Qg7 $8 30. Qe2 $1 Kh8 31. Rg4 Qf6 32. Ne4 Qf5 33. Nc3 {with a repetition of the position likely.} )29. Qf6 e5 30. Qf7+ {The ending after} (30. Ne4 Qxf6 31. Nxf6+ Kg7 32. Nxe8+ Rxe8 $17 {is pleasant for Black.} )Kh8 31. Rf6 {Black was threatening ...?e7.} (31. Ne4 {could be met by} Qe7 {or} (31... Qe6 {or} )(31... Qd1+ 32. Kg2 Qxb1 33. Qf6+ {with a draw} ))Re7 {Certainly not} (31... Re6 $2 32. Rg6 $1 )32. Rxd6 Rxf7 33. Rxh6+ Kg8 {If the ? goes to g7, White might gain a tempo by h5-h6+ later.} 34. Rxa6 Rd7 35. Bf5 Rd6 (35... Rd2 36. b4 $1 )36. Ra7 b4 37. Na4 $1 (37. Ne4 $2 Rc6 {whereas now ...?c6 can be met by ?e4} )Ne6 38. Bxe6+ Rxe6 39. Rxc7 Ra8 (39... Ra6 40. Nc5 Rxa2 41. Nd7 $1 )40. Nc5 Rh6 41. Ne4 $1 Rxa2 42. g4 Rb6 {This is the best place for this rook. Black has a threat of 43...c3 44 bxc3 b3. White must prevent it.} 43. Rxc4 $1 Rxb2 44. g5 b3 (44... b3 {Draw} 45. Rc8+ Kf7 $1 46. g6+ Kg7 47. Rc7+ Kg8 48. Rc8+ {White cannot do better than this, because of the strong passed pawn on b3.} )1/2-1/2

